+
+#### N1 candidate ranking algorithm
+
+N1 candidate discovery, visibility, and the candidate set are unchanged. Rank
+every eligible candidate by this single deterministic ordering before
+selecting the existing maximum of three suggestions:
+
+1. **Primary — spelling distance.** Use the existing Damerau–Levenshtein
+   distance between the unresolved name and the candidate's visible spelling.
+   Preserve the existing eligibility threshold.
+2. **Secondary — locality, only when spelling distances tie.** A candidate is
+   `SAME_FILE` when its declaration and the unresolved use have the same source
+   file identity; otherwise it is `CROSS_FILE`. `SAME_FILE` ranks before
+   `CROSS_FILE`. A strictly better spelling distance always ranks first,
+   regardless of locality.
+3. **Tertiary — same-file proximity.** Among `SAME_FILE` candidates, use the
+   existing declaration-proximity calculation unchanged. Source offsets are
+   compared only when both spans belong to the same file.
+4. **Tertiary — cross-file identity.** Among `CROSS_FILE` candidates, do not
+   compute declaration proximity. Compare their canonical qualified
+   declaration identities lexicographically by UTF-8 bytes, independent of
+   locale. The canonical identity is the symbol's module/type/member identity,
+   not an importing alias, import span, or user-facing spelling.
+5. **Final fallback.** If all specified keys tie, compare the visible
+   suggestion spellings lexicographically by UTF-8 bytes. This only resolves
+   an otherwise identical ranking key; it does not replace canonical identity
+   as the cross-file ordering key.
+
+The ranking MUST NOT compare source offsets from different files or use
+filesystem traversal, import-graph distance, import/source discovery order,
+hash-table iteration, operating-system directory order, build/cache order, or
+another project-wide proximity metric. The visible spelling remains the text
+shown to the programmer; canonical identity is only an ordering key. Apply the
+complete ordering before top-three truncation. This rule applies uniformly to
+ordinary imports, aliases, inherited members, and prelude candidates; it adds
+no diagnostic code or candidate-discovery mechanism.
 
